{:edb/db-before-t 1655 :edb/db-after-t 1656 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534968 50 #inst "2026-10-08T12:31:35.007Z" 13194139534968 true] [17592186166517 1100 "If $f(x,y)=\\cos(xy), x=t$, and $y=e^t$, compute $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}t}$." 13194139534968 false] [17592186166517 1100 "If $f(x,y)=\\cos(xy), x=t,$ and $y=e^t,$ compute $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}t}.$" 13194139534968 true] [17592186166517 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/assets/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t,$ where $x = x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}t} \n= \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy),\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t}), \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=1,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =[-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t})]\\cdot 1+[-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t})]\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t})-t{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t}) \\\\  & =-(1+t){e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534968 true] [17592186166517 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3173/Source/Images/e0bfff900fe36a4bdd1a679c435ccf0b.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t$, where $x = x(t)$ and $y=y(t)$, can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}t} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} =-y\\sin (xy), \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} =-x\\sin (xy)\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} &= -e^{t}\\sin (te^{t}), \\\\\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} &= -t\\sin (te^{t})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt} = 1, \\frac{dy}{dt} = e^{t}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{df}{dt} &= \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} \\cdot \\frac{dx}{dt} + \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dt} \\\\\n&= [- e^{t}\\sin (te^{t})] \\cdot 1 + [- t\\sin (te^{t})] \\cdot e^{t} \\\\\n&=-e^{t}\\sin (te^{t}) - te^{t}\\sin (te^{t}) \\\\\n&= - (1 + t)e^{t}\\sin (te^{t})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534968 false] [17592186166518 1100 "If $f(x,y) = 2\\sqrt{x+y}$ and $y=\\cos x$, compute $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}x}$." 13194139534968 false] [17592186166518 1100 "If $f(x,y) = 2\\sqrt{x+y}$ and $y=\\cos x,$ compute $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}x}.$" 13194139534968 true] [17592186166518 1145 "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y = y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x + y}}, \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x + y}}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} &= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x + \\cos x}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= -\\sin x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{df}{dx} &= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x + \\cos x}} + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x + \\cos x}} \\cdot (-\\sin x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x + \\cos x}} - \\frac{\\sin x}{\\sqrt{x + \\cos x}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 - \\sin x}{\\sqrt{x + \\cos x}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534968 false] [17592186166518 1145 "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y = y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+y}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534968 true] [17592186166519 1100 "Let $f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2$, where $x = s\\cos{t}$ and $y = t\\sin{s}$. Calculate $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}$." 13194139534968 false] [17592186166519 1100 "Let $f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2,$ where $x = s\\cos{t}$ and $y = t\\sin{s}.$ Calculate $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}.$" 13194139534968 true] [17592186166519 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/assets/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y = y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =-s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t} \\\\  & =(2s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(-s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡s)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡s) \\\\  & =-2{s}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2t{sin}^{2}⁡s \\\\  & =2t{sin}^{2}⁡s-2{s}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =2t{sin}^{2}⁡s-{s}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the identity $\\sin 2t = 2\\sin t\\cos t$ in the above simplification." 13194139534968 true] [17592186166519 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3173/Source/Images/6bba4306b4e5823056f9eea8e7d48a2a.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t$, where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y = y(s,t)$, can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = 2x, \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} = 2y\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} &= 2s\\cos t, \\\\\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} &= 2t\\sin s\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial t} &= -s\\sin t, \\\\\n\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial t} &= \\sin s\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial t} &= \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} \\cdot \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial t} + \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} \\cdot \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial t} \\\\\n&= (2s\\cos t)(-s\\sin t) + (2t\\sin s)(\\sin s) \\\\\n&=-2s^{2}\\sin t\\cos t + 2t\\sin^{2} s \\\\\n&= 2t\\sin^{2} s - 2s^{2}\\sin t\\cos t \\\\\n&= 2t\\sin^{2} s - s^{2}\\sin 2t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the identity\n\n$$\n\\sin 2t = 2\\sin t\\cos t\n$$\n\nin the above simplification." 13194139534968 false] [17592186166520 1100 "Let $f(x,y,z) = e^{xy + z}$, where $x = s + t$, $y = s-t$ and $z = -s^2$. Calculate $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}$." 13194139534968 false] [17592186166520 1100 "Let $f(x,y,z) = e^{xy + z},$ where $x = s + t,$ $y = s-t$ and $z = -s^2.$ Calculate $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}.$" 13194139534968 true] [17592186166520 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/assets/b593a1b60fe8c1aef2c5c229ff426db350e6296b9ecb767494283f0fa3342072.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t), y = y(s,t)$ and $z = z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial t}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x, y$ and $z\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y{e}^{xy+z},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{xy+z},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={e}^{xy+z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =(s-t){e}^{(s+t)(s-t)-{s}^{2}}=(s-t){e}^{-{t}^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =(s+t){e}^{(s+t)(s-t)-{s}^{2}}=(s+t){e}^{-{t}^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & ={e}^{(s+t)(s-t)-{s}^{2}}={e}^{-{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =1, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =-1, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂t} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =(s-t){e}^{-{t}^{2}}\\cdot 1+(s+t){e}^{-{t}^{2}}\\cdot (-1)+{e}^{-{t}^{2}}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =(s-t-s-t){e}^{-{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =-2t{e}^{-{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534968 true] [17592186166520 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3173/Source/Images/6edee879a5f94e8ebd640b97917195c1.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t$, where\n\n$$\nx=x(s,t), y = y(s,t)\n$$\n\nand $z = z(s,t)$, can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x, y$ and $z$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = ye^{xy + z}, \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} = xe^{xy + z}, \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z} = e^{xy + z}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} &= (s - t)e^{(s + t)(s - t) - s^{2}} = (s - t)e^{-t^{2}}, \\\\\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} &= (s + t)e^{(s + t)(s - t) - s^{2}} = (s + t)e^{-t^{2}}, \\\\\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z} &= e^{(s + t)(s - t) - s^{2}} = e^{-t^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$, $y$, and $z$ with respect to $t$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial t} &= 1, \\\\\n\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial t} &= -1, \\\\\n\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial t} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial t} &= (s - t)e^{-t^{2}} \\cdot 1 + (s + t)e^{-t^{2}} \\cdot (-1) + e^{-t^{2}} \\cdot 0 \\\\\n&= (s - t - s - t)e^{-t^{2}} \\\\\n&=-2te^{-t^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534968 false] [17592186166679 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323432 13194139534968 true] [17592186166679 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323439 13194139534968 true] [17592186166679 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323446 13194139534968 true] [17592186166679 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323453 13194139534968 true] [17592186166680 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323362 13194139534968 true] [17592186166680 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323460 13194139534968 true] [17592186166680 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323467 13194139534968 true] [17592186166680 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323474 13194139534968 true] [17592186166680 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323481 13194139534968 true] [17592186166681 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323369 13194139534968 true] [17592186166681 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323376 13194139534968 true] [17592186166681 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323397 13194139534968 true] [17592186166681 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323404 13194139534968 true] [17592186166682 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323383 13194139534968 true] [17592186166682 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323390 13194139534968 true] [17592186166682 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323411 13194139534968 true] [17592186166682 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323418 13194139534968 true] [17592186166682 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323425 13194139534968 true] [17592186167249 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534968 false] [17592186167249 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534968 true] [17592186167249 1100 "Let $f(x, y) = 2x + xy$, where $x = se^{s}$ and $y = te^{s}$. Calculate $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}$." 13194139534968 false] [17592186167249 1100 "Let $f(x,y)=2x+xy,$ where $x=s{e}^{s}$ and $y=t{e}^{s}.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}.$" 13194139534968 true] [17592186167249 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/assets/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2+y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2+t{e}^{s}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =s{e}^{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =0, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & ={e}^{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t} \\\\  & =(2+t{e}^{s})(0)+(s{e}^{s})({e}^{s}) \\\\  & =s{e}^{2s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534968 true] [17592186167249 1145 "Keep $s$ fixed. Then $x_t=0$, $y_t=e^s$, $f_x=2+y$, and $f_y=x$. Therefore $$f_t=f_xx_t+f_yy_t=(2+y)0+xe^s=(se^s)e^s=se^{2s}.$$" 13194139534968 false] [17592186167269 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534968 true] [17592186167269 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534968 false] [17592186167269 1100 "If $f(x, y) = \\ln (xy^{2}), x = \\cos t$ and $y = \\sin t$, compute $\\frac{df}{dt}$ for $0 < t < \\frac{\\pi }{2}$." 13194139534968 false] [17592186167269 1100 "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x{y}^{2}),x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}$ for $0<t<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" 13194139534968 true] [17592186167269 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/assets/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t,$ where $x=x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x{y}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{x},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{2xy}{x{y}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+2\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cot}⁡t-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534968 true] [17592186167269 1145 "On the given interval, $x,y>0$ and $f=\\ln x+2\\ln y$. Hence $f_x=1/x$ and $f_y=2/y$. The chain rule gives $$\\frac{df}{dt}=\\frac{-\\sin t}{\\cos t}+\\frac{2\\cos t}{\\sin t}=2\\cot t-\\tan t.$$" 13194139534968 false] [17592186323362 1017 #edb/ref 17592186323363 13194139534968 true] [17592186323362 1089 "q-170670" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323362 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534968 true] [17592186323362 1100 "If $f(x,y)=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)$ and $y={x}^{2}+1,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323362 1113 #uuid "5f59a934-73d9-59a0-87fd-8e302bd7c4c0" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323362 1145 "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+1},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{2x({x}^{2}+1)}{{x}^{2}+1}=2x, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =2x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =2x(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323363 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323364 13194139534968 true] [17592186323363 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323365 13194139534968 true] [17592186323363 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323366 13194139534968 true] [17592186323363 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323367 13194139534968 true] [17592186323363 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323368 13194139534968 true] [17592186323363 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534968 true] [17592186323363 1041 "selection" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323363 1104 #edb/ref 17592186323365 13194139534968 true] [17592186323363 1110 #uuid "1248808b-d21c-5c85-aa37-4780ff6b93c9" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323364 1034 "2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323364 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534968 true] [17592186323364 1111 #uuid "2dee9f13-07ad-55cb-bb80-42b247ea64e2" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323365 1034 "2x(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1))" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323365 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534968 true] [17592186323365 1111 #uuid "5503226d-87e2-54eb-9e50-a818b226e1b3" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323366 1034 "({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323366 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534968 true] [17592186323366 1111 #uuid "fe3ac4e0-14c7-54ae-88bd-5ecb81e7ef51" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323367 1034 "\\frac{2x({x}^{2}+1)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)}" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323367 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534968 true] [17592186323367 1111 #uuid "8d4dcfd8-83bb-59c3-9e36-6ada690d5227" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323368 1034 "\\frac{2x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)}" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323368 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534968 true] [17592186323368 1111 #uuid "d805b7ed-9f1e-54e8-8d09-ad8b971836b1" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323369 1017 #edb/ref 17592186323370 13194139534968 true] [17592186323369 1089 "q-170672" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323369 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534968 true] [17592186323369 1100 "Let $f(x,y)=xy,$ where $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡s$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}.$" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323369 1113 #uuid "72fe9af3-c2e0-5976-bfcc-a799e0eb9da3" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323369 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/assets/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡s)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323370 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323371 13194139534968 true] [17592186323370 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323372 13194139534968 true] [17592186323370 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323373 13194139534968 true] [17592186323370 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323374 13194139534968 true] [17592186323370 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323375 13194139534968 true] [17592186323370 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534968 true] [17592186323370 1041 "selection" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323370 1104 #edb/ref 17592186323371 13194139534968 true] [17592186323370 1110 #uuid "84b4ac1d-2e3f-5ec8-8f4b-c490be8bf844" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323371 1034 "{cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323371 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534968 true] [17592186323371 1111 #uuid "fefdc81b-b940-5346-8434-9bb289e3df35" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323372 1034 "1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323372 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534968 true] [17592186323372 1111 #uuid "a85a4d02-7eb5-575e-b06e-f8e362ea6849" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323373 1034 "{cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323373 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534968 true] [17592186323373 1111 #uuid "6405ecbc-222a-575f-93ac-06c22108490d" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323374 1034 "{cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323374 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534968 true] [17592186323374 1111 #uuid "33e380ca-0c8d-5ab7-96da-a6873393bc89" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323375 1034 "1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323375 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534968 true] [17592186323375 1111 #uuid "3562ae33-e733-5f05-95c6-3021c2597542" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323376 1017 #edb/ref 17592186323377 13194139534968 true] [17592186323376 1089 "q-170673" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323376 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534968 true] [17592186323376 1100 "Let $f(x,y)=y{e}^{x},$ where $x=t+s$ and $y=ts.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323376 1113 #uuid "c416fc6f-25fb-59a7-937c-9a9140fd2668" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323376 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/assets/21cec766fe72965697304ef4288ffb2b090ddfca179210165dbcfd42ec490b27.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y{e}^{x},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =ts{e}^{t+s}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & ={e}^{t+s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =1, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s} \\\\  & =(ts{e}^{t+s})(1)+({e}^{t+s})(t) \\\\  & =t{e}^{t+s}(s+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323377 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323378 13194139534968 true] [17592186323377 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323379 13194139534968 true] [17592186323377 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323380 13194139534968 true] [17592186323377 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323381 13194139534968 true] [17592186323377 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323382 13194139534968 true] [17592186323377 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534968 true] [17592186323377 1041 "selection" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323377 1104 #edb/ref 17592186323378 13194139534968 true] [17592186323377 1110 #uuid "f844cd4b-5331-5e19-acfc-7ca2fa0e2a25" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323378 1034 "t{e}^{t+s}(s+1)" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323378 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534968 true] [17592186323378 1111 #uuid "ceb1fd12-520c-524b-b4eb-036ae852b780" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323379 1034 "{e}^{t+s}(s+t)" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323379 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534968 true] [17592186323379 1111 #uuid "faa41d7b-47a0-59cd-87bf-b5da7b31df9f" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323380 1034 "s{e}^{t+s}(t+1)" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323380 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534968 true] [17592186323380 1111 #uuid "6330caa9-b759-5795-9b4f-8e5074f0a48e" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323381 1034 "t{e}^{t+s}(s-t)" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323381 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534968 true] [17592186323381 1111 #uuid "6346cf87-c2aa-5b83-a56f-bf202aac93d8" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323382 1034 "s{e}^{t+s}" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323382 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534968 true] [17592186323382 1111 #uuid "0dc896cc-5dd8-5e11-b2e5-84684d6fcf6b" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323383 1017 #edb/ref 17592186323384 13194139534968 true] [17592186323383 1089 "q-170675" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323383 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534968 true] [17592186323383 1100 "Let $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{y+z}{x},$ where $x=s+t,$ $y={s}^{2}$ and $z={t}^{2}.$ Given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{{c}_{1}{s}^{2}+{c}_{2}st+{c}_{3}{t}^{2}}{(s+t)^{2}},\n$$\n\nfind the value of ${c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+{c}_{3}.$" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323383 1113 #uuid "c0285d27-5a91-54ee-863f-f3912e6a5c27" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323383 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044606/assets/b593a1b60fe8c1aef2c5c229ff426db350e6296b9ecb767494283f0fa3342072.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-\\frac{y+z}{{x}^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{x},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-\\frac{{s}^{2}+{t}^{2}}{(s+t)^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{s+t}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{1}{s+t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =1, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =0, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂t} & =2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =-\\frac{{s}^{2}+{t}^{2}}{(s+t)^{2}}\\cdot (1)+\\frac{1}{s+t}\\cdot 0+\\frac{1}{s+t}\\cdot 2t \\\\  & =\\frac{-({s}^{2}+{t}^{2})+2t(s+t)}{(s+t