t{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+y),\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{x}), \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{x})+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{x}))\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1-2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{3}+1}{x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323475 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323476 13194139534968 true] [17592186323475 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323477 13194139534968 true] [17592186323475 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323478 13194139534968 true] [17592186323475 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323479 13194139534968 true] [17592186323475 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323480 13194139534968 true] [17592186323475 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534968 true] [17592186323475 1041 "selection" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323475 1104 #edb/ref 17592186323479 13194139534968 true] [17592186323475 1110 #uuid "7998fd39-de54-51f7-a96d-5992b98cd6d3" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323476 1034 "(\\frac{2{x}^{2}+1}{x})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{3}+1}{x})" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323476 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534968 true] [17592186323476 1111 #uuid "2186afd0-6435-5dce-aca4-a0e5103dfd15" 13194139534968 true] [17592186323477 1034 "(1-3{x}^{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\fr